# [N-Queens](https://leetcode.com/problems/n-queens/)

## Problem Approach

The N-Queens problem asks us to place `N` queens on an `N × N` chessboard such that no two queens attack each other.

A queen can attack another queen if they share the same **row, column, or diagonal**. We need to find all possible valid arrangements and return each arrangement as a list of strings, where `'Q'` represents a queen and `'.'` represents an empty cell.

## Theory

We use **Backtracking** to solve this problem.

1. Initialize an `N × N` chessboard with `'.'` in every cell.
2. Place exactly one queen in each row.
3. Before placing a queen at a position `(row, col)`, check whether the position is safe by verifying its column and diagonals.
4. If the position is safe, place the queen and recursively move to the next row.
5. If all `N` queens are placed successfully, convert the board into a list of strings and store the arrangement.
6. After the recursive call, remove the queen to backtrack and explore other possible arrangements.

Since we place one queen per row, we do not need to check the entire row for conflicts. We only need to check the column and diagonals.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<string>> ans;

    void convert(vector<vector<char>>& board) {
        vector<string> temp;

        for(int i = 0; i < board.size(); i++) {
            string s = "";
            for(int j = 0; j < board[0].size(); j++) {
                s += board[i][j];
            }
            temp.push_back(s);
        }

        ans.push_back(temp);
    }

    bool isValid(vector<vector<char>>& board, int row, int col) {
        int r = board.size(), c = board[0].size();

        for(int i = 0; i < r; i++)
            if(board[i][col] == 'Q') return false;

        for(int i = 0; i < c; i++)
            if(board[row][i] == 'Q') return false;

        for(int i = row, j = col; i >= 0 && j >= 0; i--, j--)
            if(board[i][j] == 'Q') return false;

        for(int i = row, j = col; i < r && j < c; i++, j++)
            if(board[i][j] == 'Q') return false;

        for(int i = row, j = col; i >= 0 && j < c; i--, j++)
            if(board[i][j] == 'Q') return false;

        for(int i = row, j = col; i < r && j >= 0; i++, j--)
            if(board[i][j] == 'Q') return false;

        return true;
    }

    void solve(vector<vector<char>>& board, int queens, int row) {
        if(queens == 0) {
            convert(board);
            return;
        }

        for(int j = 0; j < board[0].size(); j++) {
            if(isValid(board, row, j)) {
                board[row][j] = 'Q';

                solve(board, queens - 1, row + 1);

                board[row][j] = '.';
            }
        }
    }

    vector<vector<string>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        ans.clear();
        vector<vector<char>> board(n, vector<char>(n, '.'));

        solve(board, n, 0);

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** Approximately \(O(N!)\) for the backtracking search, ignoring the cost of copying completed boards.
- **Auxiliary Space Complexity:** \(O(N^2)\) for the chessboard, plus \(O(N)\) recursion stack space.
- **Output Space:** Depends on the number of valid arrangements and the size of each stored board.
